IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35): THE DATES THE TEXT FIXES.
--
-- Two dates are given by the text itself: the date of enactment under the title, and the date
-- 2 May 2022 in paragraph 1 of Schedule 2.  Commencement is left by section 15(1) to an order,
-- so no commencement date is carried here; section 15 is encoded in fraud-s15.l4 and takes
-- the date appointed as an input.

§ `Dates`

-- The stable prelude has no YMD, so this local shim (the Commons guide, section 5) spells a date
-- year first.  Date takes the day first.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YMD y m d MEANS Date d m y

§§ `Date of enactment`

-- The date in square brackets under the title.  The text does not say what the bracketed date
-- is called; it is carried as the date of enactment (fork F17 in NOTES.md), the name an Act's
-- bracketed date usually bears, which is a convention and not something this text states.
-- src:4 | [8th November 2006]
@ref Fraud Act 2006, date of enactment (src:4)
GIVETH A DATE
`the date of enactment of the Fraud Act 2006` MEANS YMD 2006 11 8

§§ `Schedule 2, paragraph 1: 2 May 2022`

-- Schedule 2 paragraph 1: for an offence committed before this date, the references to the
-- general limit in a magistrates' court in sections 1(3)(a), 6(2)(a), 7(2)(a), 9(6)(a) and
-- 11(3)(a) are read as references to 6 months.  The rule that uses it is in fraud-penalty.l4.
-- src:343 | Maximum term of imprisonment for offences under this Act
-- src:344 | 1 In relation to an offence committed before 2 May 2022, the references to the general limit in a magistrates’ court in sections 1(3)(a), 6(2)(a), 7(2)(a), 9(6)(a) and 11(3)(a) are to be read as references to 6 months.
@ref Fraud Act 2006, Schedule 2 paragraph 1 (src:344)
GIVETH A DATE
`Schedule 2 paragraph 1: the date from which the general limit is not read as 6 months` MEANS YMD 2022 5 2
